  "content": {
    "title": "Taylor Frankie Paul’s ‘Bachelorette’ Controversy, Explained",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTOn Thursday, ABC announced that it is pulling the new season of the dating show after a video of Paul attacking her ex-boyfriend surfaced.Listen · 4:53 min Taylor Frankie Paul, a star of “The Secret Lives of Mormon Wives,” would have been the rare Bachelorette to bring her own fame to the ABC franchise.Credit...John Fleenor/DisneyPublished March 18, 2026Updated March 20, 2026“The Bachelorette,” a mainstay of ABC’s dating show ecosystem, has been slipping in ratings and relevance since its peak in the late 2010s. So the announcement last fall that Taylor Frankie Paul, of Hulu’s juggernaut reality show “The Secret Lives of Mormon Wives,” would have a run this season as “The Bachelorette” came as a jolt. (The show’s tagline this season: “If you don’t fit the mold, break it.”)On Thursday, just three days before the season was scheduled to debut, the network announced that Paul’s season wouldn’t be airing after TMZ released a video showing her attacking her ex-boyfriend Dakota Mortensen in 2023.“In light of the newly released video just surfaced today, we have made the decision to not move forward with the new season of ‘The Bachelorette’ at this time, and our focus is on supporting the family,” a Disney Entertainment spokesperson said.Paul brought the show negative attention earlier this month with the news that she and Mortensen, the father of her third child, are being investigated by police on allegations of domestic violence. The Police Department in Draper City, Utah, said this week that the encounter in question occurred after Paul had wrapped “The Bachelorette,” but during filming for the upcoming season of “Mormon Wives,” which has since been paused.Cinnabon dropped Paul and her fellow “Secret Lives of Mormon Wives” cast members from a brand deal, saying the sponsorship “no longer aligns with our brand values,” and even before the video emerged, there were calls for ABC to cancel or postpone “The Bachelorette.”When Paul appeared on “Good Morning America” on Wednesday morning to promote the dating show, she said she would address the legal investigation “when the time is right.”Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Shivani Gonzalez is a news assistant at The Times who writes a weekly TV column and contributes to a variety of sections.See more on: HuluAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
